<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Iceland</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Republic of Iceland, Ísland</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Iceland is a Nordic island country in the North Atlantic. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landnamabok">Landnámabók</a>, settlement began in 874 when the Norwegian chieftain Ingólfr Arnarson became the first permanent settler. The island was governed as an independent commonwealth under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/althing">Althing</a>, until it acceded to Norwegian rule in the 13th century. It came under Danish rule after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalmar-union">Kalmar Union</a> dissolved in 1523, and the Treaty of Kiel ceded it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> in 1814. A national independence movement led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danish-icelandic-act-of-union">Danish–Icelandic Act of Union</a>, signed on 1 December 1918, which made Iceland a sovereign kingdom in personal union with Denmark. Icelanders voted in 1944 to become a republic.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/iceland.jpg" alt="Iceland" width="330" height="238" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original: Matthías Þórðarson Vector: Árni Dagur and Magasjukur2 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Iceland.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landnamabok">Landnámabók</a> names Naddodd as the first Norseman to reach the island in the 9th century; he called it Snæland, meaning Snowland, and the Swedish explorer Garðar Svavarsson, who sailed round it, named it Garðarshólmur. The Landnámabók and the Íslendingabók say that monks known as the Papar lived there before the Scandinavian settlers, possibly members of a Hiberno-Scottish mission. Excavation at Hafnir on the Reykjanes peninsula found a cabin abandoned sometime between 770 and 880. Genetic study suggests that most male settlers were of Nordic origin and most women of Gaelic origin, so that many settlers were Norsemen who brought Gaelic slaves.</p><p>The Commonwealth, established in the 10th century, held its assembly, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/althing">Althing</a>, from 930. It fell into violent conflict among chieftains, chiefly the Sturlung family, in the Age of the Sturlungs (c. 1220–1264), and the signing of the Old Covenant in 1262–1264 brought Iceland under Norwegian rule. Iceland joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalmar-union">Kalmar Union</a> in 1397, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a> on it in 1550. The Althing was suspended from 1799 to 1845, and the modern parliament, founded in 1845 as an advisory body to the Danish monarch, was widely seen as a re-establishment of the earlier assembly.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danish-icelandic-act-of-union">Danish–Icelandic Act of Union</a>, signed on 1 December 1918 for 25 years, recognised Iceland as a sovereign state in personal union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, which handled certain defence and foreign affairs on its behalf. It expired on 31 December 1943. In a four-day plebiscite beginning on 20 May 1944, 97 per cent voted to end the union and 95 per cent approved a republican constitution. Iceland became a republic on 17 June 1944, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sveinn-bjornsson">Sveinn Björnsson</a> as first president.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Iceland lies on the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, where the Eurasian and North American plates move apart, and its main island lies entirely south of the Arctic Circle. Reykjavík is the capital and largest city, home to about 35 per cent of the roughly 395,000 residents. The official language is Icelandic, which has changed less from Old Norse than other Nordic languages. The state has no standing army, only a coast guard, and the Lutheran Church of Iceland is the state church under the constitution. After the failure of its three largest banks in 2008, parliament passed emergency legislation in October that year.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Iceland” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Denmark and Norway thus remained under one king of the house of Oldenburg, together with <b>Iceland</b>, the Faroe Islands and the Norse claims to Greenland, while the king also ruled the duchies of Schleswig and Holstein.</blockquote><a href="/empire/denmark-norway">Denmark-Norway · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the sixteenth century Bremen ships traded as far as <b>Iceland</b> and Shetland.</blockquote><a href="/empire/free-hanseatic-city-of-bremen">Free Hanseatic City of Bremen · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The union of crowns with Norway from 1380 to 1814 made Danish the written language of Norway, and <b>Iceland</b>, the Faroe Islands and Greenland stayed under the Danish crown after Norway’s departure, so that Danish administration and language shaped the whole North Atlantic.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-denmark">Kingdom of Denmark · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Norwegians settled the Faroe Islands, <b>Iceland</b>, Shetland, Orkney, the Hebrides and Man, raided and settled in Ireland and northern Britain, and from Iceland reached Greenland and, around 1000, Newfoundland, where the Norse site at L'Anse aux Meadows has been excavated.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-norway">Kingdom of Norway · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Corsairs from Algiers and Salé raided <b>Iceland</b> in 1627, and in 1631 a raid led by the Dutch renegade Jan Janszoon, known as Murat Reis, sacked Baltimore in Ireland.</blockquote><a href="/empire/regency-of-algiers">Regency of Algiers · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The richest source is the Poetic Edda, a collection of anonymous poems about the gods and heroes preserved mainly in a single Icelandic manuscript, the Codex Regius, written around 1270, which Denmark returned to <b>Iceland</b> in 1971.</blockquote><a href="/religion/norse-religion">Norse religion · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li></ul></section>
